{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1337303-help-for-a-newbie","id":1337303,"title":"Help for a newbie","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-09-22T01:31:40-07:00","id":1337303,"title":"Help for a newbie","content":"Hey!<br /> I'm running a vampire chronicle in ancient Rome. I want to add some Mage elements, as NPC associated with PC's.<br /> I've a pretty basic knowledge of modern MTA and many questions.<br /> <br /> Back in that time, before the traditions, did magic worked with spheres? Or were there other rules sets for magic?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11010-ersito","name":"erSito","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11010_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-22T12:00:30-07:00","id":1337343,"title":"","content":"There were other rule sets, as presented in Dark Ages: Mage (and V20 Black Hand). The mage groups that existed before the Traditions, the Fellowships, each had a Foundation (taking the place of Arete) and four Pillars (taking the place of Spheres, but less versatile).<br /> <br /> Taking the Itarajana in V20 Black Hand as an example, their Foundation is Dharma, and their Pillars are Naraka (dealing with destruction, heat, cold, curses and matters infernal), Preta (dealing with chaos, death, ghosts and the Underworld), Triyagyoni (dealing with nature, life, passions, the Middle Umbra and spirits), and Deva (dealing with Quintessence, creation, correspondences and thought).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-22T12:32:27-07:00","id":1337349,"title":"","content":"The down side of the Dark Ages system being that each Foundation + Pillars set is designed for that group and can, at best, be cludged into a different one. And if none of them fit what you need - the Cult of Bacchus or the Persphene Kore or Roman street magicians - then you have to invent your own. <br /> <br /> My advice is always to keep things as simple as possible, especially when it comes to NPCs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 09-22-2019, 03:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-25T05:07:15-07:00","id":1337793,"title":"","content":"My advice would be the same, and I would add that there's absolutely no need to bother with the Pillar System if you don't want, because Spheres can be used <b>and were used</b> to represent magick back then.<br /> <br /> As proof: Dead Magic uses both, Pillars and Spheres, to represent ancient magick. And the Etruscan Lich (much older than the DA) uses Spheres to represent his magick. <hr /><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>erSito</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Back in that time, before the traditions, did magic worked with spheres? Or were there other rules sets for magic?</div> </div> </div> </div>One needs to differentiate from System and Game World here (I'm always repeating this: One thing it's the system of a game, and other thing it's the lore of a game. The fluff and the crunch).<br /> <br /> In the Lore, Spheres were discovered (or, perhaps, re-discovered) in the Grand Convocation after the Dark Ages. As <i>marin </i>said, DA has a diferent system to represent magick before this point in time (but there's no proof that the Foundation/Pillar system was the first system of magick ever, and there is evidence that points that Spheres or a very similar system may have been used in Egypt before).<br /> <br /> Spheres as a game system, however, were used to represent magick back then several times in the canon (as I mentioned).<br /> <br /> So you can use them if you don't want to invent Foundations for the different Practices back then. Also, using Spheres allows for compatibility with M20, which Pillars don't have. <hr />If you can get Dead Magic (I think it's Dead Magic 1), it talks about magick and the Traditions in ancient Rome and Greece (Especially the Cult of Mercury, the predecessors of the OoH).<br /> <br /> If I'm not mistaken, it uses Spheres to describe the effects (I don't remember if it also uses Pillars, but I think only Dead Magic 2 has them both).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 09-25-2019, 08:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-25T09:16:38-07:00","id":1337816,"title":"","content":"Dead Magick 1 uses spheres to describe effects. The Sorcerer's Crusade books also use spheres.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}